[DOC title="@language/Patterns"]

	A `[PATTERN]` documents **one composition** — a user menu, a notifications
	system, a comment thread. The level between a component and a page: bigger
	than a button, smaller than a screen, and the level most design systems
	never write down.

	It is a `[SHOWCASE]` with the prop half switched off. A composition has no
	single component whose API it could extract, so there is no `[COMPONENT]`,
	no props panel and no controls — a props table documenting one part of a
	composition would be actively misleading. The body **is** the composition.

	````sdoc
	<script lang="ts">
		import Avatar from './Avatar.svelte';
		import Menu from './Menu.svelte';
	</script>

	[PATTERN title="Patterns / User Menu" description="Avatar, menu and badge."]

		<Avatar name="Ada" /> <Menu>Profile · Settings · Sign out</Menu>

	[/PATTERN]
	````

	The page is the title, the description, the composition on a stage, and its
	source in a code panel — because a pattern is a thing you copy.

	## Attributes

	| Attribute | Required | Meaning |
	|---|---|---|
	| `title` | yes | Sidebar path and display name — `"Patterns / User Menu"` nests under Patterns |
	| `description` | no | Short text under the title — inline markdown renders styled |
	| `slug` | no | Overrides the URL segment (default: slugified title segment) |
	| `hide` | no | A bare flag: routable, but never listed in a sidebar |
	| `maxWidth` | no | Stage width (default `100%`) |
	| `padding` | no | Stage padding |
	| `direction` | no | Stage `flex-direction` |
	| `gap` | no | Stage `gap` |
	| `contentX` | no | Horizontal alignment: `left`/`center`/`right`/`justify` |
	| `contentY` | no | Vertical alignment: `top`/`middle`/`bottom`/`justify` |
	| `background` | no | Stage background — a CSS color or a `var()` from the project's css |
	| `minHeight` | no | Minimum stage height |

	## The body is Svelte, in your project's context

	The stage loads the project's [`css`](/explorer/configuration#css), the same
	way a `[COMPONENT]` preview and an `[EXAMPLE]` do — the composition looks
	like your product, not like these docs. An entity-level `<script>` and
	`<style>` work as they do elsewhere: the script's imports are what the body
	composes from, and the style joins the stage's css.

	## It takes no blocks

	A pattern is one composition, so `[COMPONENT]`, `[EXAMPLE]`, `[NOTES]`,
	`[TODO]`, `[PROSE]` and `[GLOSSARY]` are not read inside one — its body is
	Svelte markup, and a block written there would render as literal text.
	Rather than let that pass silently, each is reported:

	```
	error  src/UserMenu.sdoc:3 › Patterns / User Menu
	  [COMPONENT] is not read inside a [PATTERN] — a pattern is one
	  composition, written directly in its body. For a component with its API
	  and variants, use [SHOWCASE].
	```

	## When to reach for which

	- **[`[PATTERN]`](/language/patterns)** — several components assembled into
	  one reusable thing. No single API to document.
	- **[`[SHOWCASE]`](/language/component-docs)** — one component, with its
	  extracted API, live controls and as many variants as you like.
	- **[`[LAYOUT]`](/language/layout-docs)** — a whole page rather than a piece
	  of one, on a full-bleed canvas you can resize to real viewport widths.
	- **[`[DOC]`](/language/doc-pages)** — prose that isn't tied to one thing.

	If a pattern needs several *states* — signed in and signed out, an empty
	list and a full one — document them as separate patterns, or use a
	`[SHOWCASE]` with one `[EXAMPLE]` per state.

	## See also

	- [Component docs](/language/component-docs) — `[SHOWCASE]` and `[COMPONENT]`
	- [Layouts](/language/layout-docs) — full-page compositions
	- [Overview](/language/overview) — every entity, side by side

[/DOC]
